<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=paper-money">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Communication and computing</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paper money</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Paper money is currency printed on paper rather than minted in metal. It was first developed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a>, beginning with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiaozi">jiaozi</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> and later standardised as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huizi">huizi</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty</a> continued its use widely, but over-issue later contributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paper-money.jpg" alt="Paper money" width="308" height="537" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jiao_zi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Paper money, often called a note or a bill, is a negotiable promissory note payable to the bearer on demand. Its main kinds are government notes, issued directly by political authorities, and banknotes, issued by banks of issue such as central banks; merchants in pre-modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> issued it too. It is often, though not always, legal tender. It is lighter to carry than coins but less durable, and counterfeiting has been a constant problem, driving the development of security <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> in recent centuries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Its roots lie in the merchant receipts of deposit of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a> (618–907). Strings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> coins were too heavy for large transactions, so a merchant left his coins with a trusted person and received a slip recording the deposit, which won the coins back when returned. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> the central government granted several deposit shops a monopoly of issuing such certificates, and by the 1120s it was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> its own notes from woodblocks.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> state ran paper money factories at Huizhou, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangzhou">Hangzhou</a> and Anqi; in 1175 the one at Hangzhou employed more than a thousand workers a day. Notes were at first valid only in designated regions and for three years. From 1107 they were printed in at least six ink colours, with intricate designs to deter forgers, and between 1265 and 1274 the late Southern Song introduced a national paper currency backed by gold or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a>, issued notes called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiaochao">Jiaochao</a>, and travellers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-of-rubruck">William of Rubruck</a> made Chinese paper money known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. In medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> money traders used promissory notes, and the term bank note comes from the 14th-century nota di banco. In the mid-17th century the goldsmith bankers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> made their receipts payable to the bearer, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholms-banco">Stockholms Banco</a> issued banknotes in 1661 before going bankrupt three years later.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bank-of-england">Bank of England</a>, founded in 1694, began the permanent issue of banknotes in 1695, and the Bank of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> in 1696 became the first European bank to issue them in fixed values. The Bank of England Act 1833 established that banknotes would be legal tender in peacetime, and the Bank Charter Act 1844 restricted the authority to issue new notes to the Bank of England.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Paper money” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Jin issued <b>paper money</b> from the 1150s; heavy issues to pay for wars in the thirteenth century caused severe inflation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/jin-dynasty-1115-1234">Jin dynasty (1115-1234) · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Song economy was the most commercially developed the world had yet seen, with widespread use of <b>paper money</b>, extensive domestic and maritime trade, proto-industrial production and an urban commercial culture that changed Chinese society.</blockquote><a href="/empire/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Marco Polo's celebrated description of China's commerce, with its <b>paper money</b>, canals, great cities and abundance of goods, reflects the real vitality of Yuan China, though it also reflects the wonder of a foreign merchant meeting a society far more commercially developed than any in contemporary Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Paper money</b>, which began as merchants’ deposit receipts in Sichuan and was issued by the government from 1024, eased their trade.</blockquote><a href="/societies/song-china">Social Hierarchy of Song China</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Paper money</b>, the jiaochao, issued from 1260, circulated across the empire, though over-issue later brought inflation.</blockquote><a href="/societies/yuan-china">Social Hierarchy of Yuan China</a></li><li><blockquote>To pay soldiers back from a failed expedition against Quebec, Massachusetts issued bills of credit, the first government <b>paper money</b> in the Americas (dated 1690 under the Old Style calendar).</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-3">3 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1023</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Sichuan, China</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/empire/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Merchants in Sichuan issued the first notes, the jiaozi; the government took over their production in 1023.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/papermaking">Papermaking<span>179–141 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/woodblock-printing">Woodblock printing<span>before 220 CE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=paper-money">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
